WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.740
Pitch to buyers and funders, online and in person.

2
00:00:04.090 --> 00:00:07.530
This is the video for module four of Presentation Skills.

3
00:00:08.780 --> 00:00:17.050
A buyer is not asking, is this a nice product? She is asking, will this sell, arrive on time, and make me money without problems?

4
00:00:18.300 --> 00:00:23.960
A funder wants to know: is the problem real, can you deliver, and will the money be used well?

5
00:00:25.210 --> 00:00:28.790
More pitches now happen online, and that needs extra care.

6
00:00:30.040 --> 00:00:35.010
And your skill grows only through real talks. So you will plan your next 90 days.

7
00:00:36.260 --> 00:00:43.070
First, visit the shop. See what similar products they stock, what they charge, and what is missing from the shelf.

8
00:00:44.320 --> 00:00:48.200
Open with a need you noticed in their shop, then your one-line offer.

9
00:00:49.450 --> 00:00:54.590
Show the product. Let them see, touch or taste it, and give sizes and packaging.

10
00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:59.190
Give proof: who already buys, and how often they reorder.

11
00:01:00.440 --> 00:01:06.990
Make a clear offer: price to them, suggested shelf price, minimum order, delivery days and payment terms.

12
00:01:08.240 --> 00:01:17.410
Then ask for a specific trial order. If you are unsure you can supply it, say: let me confirm with my production schedule and call you by tomorrow.

13
00:01:18.660 --> 00:01:29.370
Buyers think in margin: the difference between what they pay you and what they sell for. Adaeze noticed the chiller had no local drinks, and hands the manager a cold bottle.

14
00:01:30.620 --> 00:01:33.990
She suggests a shelf price of 500 naira a bottle.

15
00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:37.850
Her price to the shop is 400 naira.

16
00:01:39.100 --> 00:01:45.600
So the shop earns 100 naira on every bottle. Her own price must still cover her costs and profit.

17
00:01:45.950 --> 00:01:53.700
She asks for a trial of 48 bottles. The manager agrees to 24, and she confirms it by WhatsApp the same day.

18
00:01:54.950 --> 00:02:00.600
A funding pitch has six parts. The problem, and how you know it is real. Then your solution.

19
00:02:01.850 --> 00:02:11.280
Your evidence. For example, Rukayat's shea group in Ilorin had already trained 15 members and sold small batches, and she showed the sales book.

20
00:02:12.530 --> 00:02:19.680
Your plan and budget. Base each line on real quotes, tie it to a result, and show what you contribute yourself.

21
00:02:20.930 --> 00:02:27.650
Your team, and how money will be tracked: two signatories, and regular reports with photos and sales records.

22
00:02:28.900 --> 00:02:38.580
And a precise ask. Rukayat asked for 900,000 naira to set up the unit within three months, and offered to send the full proposal that week.

23
00:02:39.830 --> 00:02:44.500
A grant does not need to be paid back, but it usually needs detailed reports.

24
00:02:45.750 --> 00:02:54.860
A loan must be repaid with interest, so a lender looks at your cash flow. Never borrow more than you can repay, and read every term before signing.

25
00:02:56.110 --> 00:03:09.280
Be careful of anyone who asks for a fee to release a grant, or promises guaranteed funding on social media. Real funders publish how to apply. Never share your bank PIN or OTP.

26
00:03:10.530 --> 00:03:19.490
Online, prop your phone so the camera is at eye level. Face a window or lamp, never with a bright window behind you. Choose a quiet room.

27
00:03:20.740 --> 00:03:29.720
Share only the window with your slides, so private messages stay hidden, and switch on Do Not Disturb. Keep it short and use people's names.

28
00:03:30.070 --> 00:03:35.700
Send a PDF summary the day before, so members with poor connections still get the content.

29
00:03:36.950 --> 00:03:43.690
Choose three real opportunities. One low-pressure, such as an announcement at church or your association.

30
00:03:44.940 --> 00:03:47.950
One medium, such as a report or short training.

31
00:03:49.200 --> 00:03:55.260
And one high-stakes buyer or funder pitch. Put the dates in your calendar and prepare two weeks ahead.

32
00:03:56.510 --> 00:04:04.290
After each talk, write in your speaker log: your message, the time you used, the result, and one thing to keep and one to change.

33
00:04:05.540 --> 00:04:19.730
To remember. Study the shop first. Pitch with hook, product, proof, offer and ask. Show the buyer's margin while protecting your profit. Tie every budget line to a result. And never pay a fee to receive a grant.

34
00:04:20.980 --> 00:04:29.110
Now prepare a buyer pitch for a real shop, draft a funding pitch with a 60-second version, and write your 90-day speaking plan.

35
00:04:29.460 --> 00:04:31.400
Continue with the lessons below.
